467 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 157 0 R
/T (Before creating our own control system, we first learned how the BaseBot behaved using the default joystick mapping provided in the NRL programming guide. The PS5 controller connected to the ESP32 through Bluetooth, and each input was assigned a clear, predefined function. The left joystick controlled forward and backward movement, allowing the robot to accelerate smoothly across the field. The right joystick handled turning, making it possible to rotate or adjust direction with small, precise movements. The basic functional buttons controlled the arm and gripper actions using simple servo commands, giving the robot its ability to lift and release objects.)
/E (Before creating our own control system, we first learned how the BaseBot behaved using the default joystick mapping provided in the NRL programming guide. The PS5 controller connected to the ESP32 through Bluetooth, and each input was assigned a clear, predefined function. The left joystick controlled forward and backward movement, allowing the robot to accelerate smoothly across the field. The right joystick handled turning, making it possible to rotate or adjust direction with small, precise movements. The basic functional buttons controlled the arm and gripper actions using simple servo commands, giving the robot its ability to lift and release objects.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
